WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.260
[Baby Laughing]

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:03.320
Paid family leave.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:06.450
It’s what moments like these are all about.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.720
Do you support a nationwide paid parental leave policy?

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.255
Absolutely and even more.

00:00:11.255 --> 00:00:12.269
[Applause]

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:15.990
Politicians always want government to do more.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.640
Now they want to force companies to give

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.040
every parent paid time off.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.580
Every worker in America

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.230
should be guaranteed at least 12 weeks

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.570
Every worker should get at least 12 weeks.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:29.570
Why 12 weeks?

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.540
That's a very arbitrary number.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.030
Why not 14 weeks?

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.769
Why not 26 weeks?

00:00:34.769 --> 00:00:36.370
Onwuka is a working mom.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:39.400
Bonding time between a mom and a baby is important.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.120
When she had her son, her employer,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.820
the Independent Women’s Forum,

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.540
gave her 6 weeks paid leave.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.309
But she wanted more time off.

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:50.020
I supplemented those six weeks with additional time off,

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.200
some vacation time.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.200
This is my baby boy Jesse.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:54.820
It was wonderful to have that time off,

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.840
but it's also important for me to be able to customize

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.110
the time off that I needed.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:02.420
Government programs however are hard to customize,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:07.260
but now both parties are pushing government solutions.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.140
This is not a women’s issue.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.030
It’s a family issue.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.540
And America, say people like John Oliver,

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.580
is way behind the world.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.840
We and Papua New Guinea are the only countries

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.660
in the world that do not provide any paid time off.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.680
Just us and Papua New Guinea.

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:28.140
It's disingenuous to say, uh-oh,

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.560
we're the only nation along with Papa New Guinea.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.940
Because most American workers already get paid leave.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.640
17% get parental leave.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.100
But you jumped to 60, 70, 80% when you consider

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.520
people have sick time off, overtime, or an

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.690
all-encompassing personal time.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.920
So companies and workers are already kind

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.740
of working this out voluntarily

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:51.240
They are.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.380
Paid leave is spreading.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.140
[Walmart sounds]

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.190
And not just for high paid employees.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:59.060
We are talking about Chipotle workers, CVS workers,

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.040
Walmart workers started to get paid leave.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.080
Why would CVS and Walmart provide this voluntarily?

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.320
For an employer to attract good talent

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.720
or retain their talent, they need to offer benefits

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.860
that really resonate with workers.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.200
Paid maternity and paternity leave

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.480
is one of those benefits.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.240
It’ll be good for business.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.980
Politicians are so arrogant that they say

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:24.080
they must tell business what’s good for business.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.080
It’s good for business.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.080
It’s good for this country.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.820
In truth, it is bad for business

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.360
and not even good for most women.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.680
If we look at how the rest of the world has provided

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.360
very generous, paid leave, mandated paid leave plans,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.920
we see that it actually has a negative impact on women.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.340
Why would that be?

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.600
Because mandatory leave

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.920
makes a young woman a hiring risk.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.820
If an employer has a young woman in front of him

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.690
of child bearing age and he's thinking,

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.860
okay, I have to provide paid time off

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:53.250
for this young woman or I have a potential other

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.500
employee who's a male

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.950
The male becomes a safer bet.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.480
And sure enough, in California,

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.500
the first state to mandate paid family leave,

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.280
a study found women of childbearing age

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.349
were more likely to be unemployed.

00:03:06.349 --> 00:03:09.160
And in Europe, lots of women do work,

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.680
but in lower level jobs.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.400
American women are more likely to be in

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.940
senior level positions, managerial positions,

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.840
than women in Europe.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.240
Twice as likely?

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.240
Twice as likely.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.260
And it's very much tied to these mandates

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.430
around paid leave and paid time off.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:25.500
On top of all that

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.340
Our politicians understate the cost.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.660
It’s such a small amount to of money.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.960
It’s the cost of a cup of coffee a week.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.880
About a dollar sixty-one cents a year.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.320
But that’s not true.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.819
In California, the cost is already $12 a week.

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:44.980
And government programs always grow.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:47.560
There is a push to add a huge extension

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.770
to paid parental leave in California

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.260
Mission creep always happens

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:52.879
with government programs.

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:57.250
All of a sudden that $12 goes to $24, $36, $48

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:59.840
So can’t we just say, parental leave is something

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.960
best left to employers and employees?

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.730
Leave them free to make their own deals?

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.140
Probably not, I’m sad to report,

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:10.720
because now even politicians who call themselves

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.190
conservatives want government to do something.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:15.860
As conservatives we’re pro-family.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:18.000
He and some other Republicans support a bill

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.639
called The Cradle Act

00:04:20.639 --> 00:04:22.560
The Cradle Act would allow new parents

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.110
to tap into their Social Security savings.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.360
At least the Republican plan is not mandatory.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.260
Anyone can choose whether or not

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.639
to participate in the program.

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:34.420
Here is Onwuka at the press conference

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.199
showing her support

00:04:36.199 --> 00:04:38.780
Already Social Security is unsustainable,

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:41.490
now you're going to break it open for paid leave?

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.710
But It's not a growth of government.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.140
What's interesting about this plan

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:46.669
Yes it is, you are tapping Social Security,

00:04:46.669 --> 00:04:49.199
there'll be a new bureaucracy to monitor it.

00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:51.569
It's not a new entitlement, it's not a new bureaucracy.

00:04:51.569 --> 00:04:55.140
It is literally just allowing workers to tap

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:57.349
into Social Security benefits that they are

00:04:57.349 --> 00:04:58.639
already paying for.

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:00.520
So when they're older there'll be less in their

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:04.060
Social Security fund and there won't be enough money

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.360
and we'll have to raise taxes again.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.900
Older workers or retirees have more resources

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.100
when they retire.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:11.520
Younger workers are the ones who

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.159
have lower incomes.

00:05:13.159 --> 00:05:15.860
Give me a break, can’t we ever say there

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:17.860
is something that’s not government’s job?

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:21.140
We’re already $22 trillion in debt.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:24.200
We don’t need another government program.

